= Connectivity (graph theory) =

'''Connectivity''' in graph theory describes whether vertices of a graph can be reached from one another through paths and how resistant that reachability is to removal of vertices or edges. It is a foundational property of networks because it distinguishes one connected structure from separate components and provides formal measures of vulnerability and redundancy.

For an undirected graph <math>G=(V,E)</math>, the graph is connected when every pair of vertices is joined by a path. For directed graphs, connectivity requires additional distinctions because reachability can depend on direction.

== Paths and reachability ==

A '''path''' is a sequence of vertices connected by edges.

In an undirected graph, if a path exists from <math>u</math> to <math>v</math>, then the same edges can be traversed in reverse.

In a directed graph, a directed path from <math>u</math> to <math>v</math> does not imply a path from <math>v</math> to <math>u</math>.

Connectivity is therefore fundamentally a reachability relation.

== Connected graph ==

A nonempty undirected graph is connected if

<math>\forall u,v\in V,\quad u\leadsto v.</math>

If at least one pair has no path between them, the graph is disconnected.

A connected graph can still be poorly connected in a practical sense. One narrow bridge can make the whole graph formally connected while leaving it fragile.

== Connected components ==

A '''connected component''' is a maximal connected subgraph.

The connected components partition the vertex set:

<math>V=C_1\cup C_2\cup\cdots\cup C_k,</math>

with the components pairwise disjoint.

A graph is connected exactly when it has one connected component.

Isolated vertices form one-vertex components under standard conventions.

== Strong connectivity ==

A directed graph is '''strongly connected''' when every ordered pair of vertices is mutually reachable:

<math>u\leadsto v</math>

and

<math>v\leadsto u.</math>

Strongly connected components are maximal subgraphs satisfying this property.

They partition the vertices of any directed graph.

== Weak connectivity ==

A directed graph is '''weakly connected''' if the undirected graph formed by ignoring edge directions is connected.

Weak connectivity is much less demanding than strong connectivity.

A one-way hierarchy can be weakly connected while containing no directed cycle and no mutual reachability between many pairs.

== Semiconnectivity ==

A directed graph is '''semiconnected''' if for every pair <math>u,v</math>, there is a directed path from <math>u</math> to <math>v</math> or from <math>v</math> to <math>u</math>.

This is intermediate between weak and strong connectivity.

It is useful in some ordering and dependency problems.

== Articulation vertices ==

An '''articulation vertex''' or cut vertex is a vertex whose removal increases the number of connected components.

Such a vertex is a structural single point of failure.

A transport or communication network can be connected but highly vulnerable if key articulation points exist.

== Bridges ==

A '''bridge''' or cut edge is an edge whose removal increases the number of connected components.

Bridges are the edge analog of articulation vertices.

A tree has every edge as a bridge.

Networks with cycles can provide alternate routes and thereby reduce bridge dependence.

== Vertex connectivity ==

The '''vertex connectivity''' <math>\kappa(G)</math> is the minimum number of vertices whose removal disconnects the graph or reduces it to a trivial graph, under standard conventions.

A graph is <math>k</math>-vertex-connected if at least <math>k</math> vertices must be removed to disconnect it.

Higher vertex connectivity indicates greater resilience to node loss in the topological model.

== Edge connectivity ==

The '''edge connectivity''' <math>\lambda(G)</math> is the minimum number of edges whose removal disconnects a connected graph.

A graph is <math>k</math>-edge-connected if fewer than <math>k</math> edge removals cannot disconnect it.

For connected simple graphs with at least two vertices,

<math>\kappa(G)\le\lambda(G)\le\delta(G),</math>

where <math>\delta(G)</math> is the minimum vertex degree.<ref name="diestel">[https://link.springer.com/book/10.1007/978-3-662-53622-3 Diestel, R., ''Graph Theory'', 5th ed., Springer.]</ref>

== Menger's theorem ==

Menger's theorem links cut size with disjoint paths.

Informally, the minimum number of vertices needed to separate two nonadjacent vertices equals the maximum number of internally vertex-disjoint paths between them.

An analogous edge version equates minimum edge cut size with the maximum number of edge-disjoint paths.

This is one of the central structural results connecting resilience and path redundancy.

== Local versus global connectivity ==

'''Local connectivity''' can refer to the minimum separator between a particular pair of vertices.

'''Global connectivity''' minimizes over the graph.

A network can have high connectivity in one region and a weak bottleneck elsewhere.

One global number can therefore hide heterogeneous vulnerability.

== Blocks ==

A '''block''' is a maximal subgraph without an articulation vertex, subject to standard definitions that include certain bridge or isolated cases.

Block decomposition reveals robust regions linked through vulnerable articulation points.

It is useful for understanding network structure beyond a simple connected/disconnected label.

== Trees ==

A tree is a connected acyclic undirected graph.

Between any two vertices there is exactly one simple path.

Therefore every edge is a bridge.

Trees provide connectivity with the minimum number of edges, <math>|V|-1</math>, but no path redundancy.

== Cycles and redundancy ==

Cycles provide alternative paths.

If an edge lies on a cycle, that edge is not a bridge.

Redundant paths improve topological resilience.

However, redundancy can still share common vertices or infrastructure and therefore fail under correlated failures.

== Breadth-first search ==

Breadth-first search (BFS) explores a graph layer by layer.

Starting from one vertex, BFS reaches exactly the vertices in its connected component in an undirected graph.

With adjacency lists, runtime is

<math>O(|V|+|E|).</math>

Running BFS from each not-yet-visited vertex identifies all connected components.

== Depth-first search ==

Depth-first search (DFS) also finds connected components in linear time.

DFS has additional structural uses.

A single DFS can identify:

* articulation vertices;
* bridges;
* biconnected components.

The algorithm uses discovery times and low-link information to detect whether subtrees have alternate connections to earlier vertices.

== Strongly connected component algorithms ==

Strongly connected components can be found in

<math>O(|V|+|E|)</math>

time using algorithms such as:

* Tarjan's algorithm;
* Kosaraju–Sharir algorithm;
* path-based SCC algorithms.

Contracting each SCC into one node produces a directed acyclic graph.

This condensation graph summarizes reachability among strongly connected regions.

== Minimum cuts ==

A cut partitions vertices into two sets.

The edges crossing the partition form an edge cut.

Minimum-cut algorithms find small separating sets.

For edge connectivity between designated vertices, max-flow/min-cut methods can be used when capacities are assigned appropriately.

Global minimum cut can be computed with specialized algorithms.

== Vertex cuts through transformation ==

Vertex-capacity problems can be converted to edge-capacity problems by splitting each vertex into input and output copies joined by an edge representing vertex capacity.

This permits some vertex-connectivity questions to be solved using flow algorithms.

Care is needed with terminals and graph conventions.

== Dynamic connectivity ==

In a dynamic graph, edges or vertices are inserted and deleted over time.

Recomputing components from scratch after every update can be inefficient.

Dynamic-connectivity data structures maintain connectivity under updates.

The difficulty depends on whether updates are:

* insertion-only;
* deletion-only;
* fully dynamic.

== Temporal connectivity ==

In a temporal graph, edges exist only at certain times.

A static aggregated graph can appear connected even when no time-respecting path exists.

For communication or transport, edge order can matter.

Temporal reachability should therefore replace static connectivity when timing is essential.

== Weighted networks ==

Connectivity normally ignores edge weights.

A graph can be connected through an edge with extremely high cost, latency, or low capacity.

Practical network analysis may require:

* shortest paths;
* capacity;
* reliability;
* flow.

Topological connectivity is only one layer.

== Reliability ==

If edges fail probabilistically, network reliability asks for probability the graph remains connected.

Exact reliability computation can be difficult.

Monte Carlo simulation is often used for large networks.

Independent-failure assumptions can be unrealistic when many edges share physical infrastructure.

== Random failures and targeted attacks ==

Random node deletion and targeted removal can have very different effects.

A network robust to random failure may be fragile when high-centrality articulation or hub nodes are attacked.

Robustness claims should specify the removal model.

== Percolation ==

Percolation theory studies how large connected components appear or disappear as nodes or edges are occupied probabilistically.

A giant connected component can emerge above a threshold in large random networks.

Percolation provides a statistical connection between graph connectivity and phase-transition behavior.

Real network thresholds depend on topology and correlations.

== Connectivity and degree ==

High degree can support connectivity but does not guarantee it.

A graph can contain two dense clusters with no edge between them.

Average degree therefore cannot substitute for component analysis.

Likewise, minimum degree provides only a bound on connectivity.

== Connectivity and latency ==

Connected means reachable, not fast.

A path can contain many hops.

Diameter and shortest-path distributions measure distance.

Communication systems need both connectivity and acceptable latency.

== Connectivity and bandwidth ==

A graph can be connected through one low-capacity link.

Topological reachability says nothing about throughput.

Flow and capacity models are needed when resource volume matters.

== Knowledge graphs ==

In a [[Knowledge graph]], connectivity can describe structural paths among entities.

A path between two concepts does not imply that the relationship is meaningful, causal, or epistemically valid.

Graph structure should not be overinterpreted.

== Computer networks ==

In a [[Computer network]], connectivity can refer to physical or logical paths.

Actual communication also depends on:

* routing;
* permissions;
* protocol compatibility;
* failures.

A connected physical graph can still be operationally partitioned.

== Multi-agent systems ==

A [[Multi-agent system]] often uses a communication graph.

Connectivity conditions can determine whether agents can reach consensus or coordinate.

Time-varying agent networks may require joint connectivity over intervals rather than connectivity at each instant.

== Epistemic boundary ==

Graph connectivity is a precise topological property.

It does not establish:

* communication quality;
* causal influence;
* social trust;
* information flow;
* semantic relatedness.

Applications should state which graph the connectivity claim refers to and whether direction, time, capacity, or reliability must also be modeled.

